Die 1 is 4, so the expected sum is 4 + 3.5 + 3.5 = 11. The sum ranges from 6 to 16 and is symmetric around 11.

For an ask A, the expected edge per quote is 0.65·P(noise valuation > A)·(A − 11) − 0.35·E[(S − A)⁺], where S is the sum. Evaluating this gives roughly 0.42 at A = 13, 0.44 at 14, 0.46 at 15, and 0.42 at 16, so 15 is about the best ask. By symmetry around 11, the best bid is 7. I'm quoting 7 / 15 with a spread of 8 to cover adverse selection from the 35% informed traders.

{"bid": 7, "ask": 15}

Die 1 is 4, and dice 2 and 3 are unknown. The public fair value is 4 + 3.5 + 3.5 = **11**, and no trades have happened yet, so nothing else updates it.

Quoting symmetrically around 11, I compared expected profit per ask level. Noise buyers pay more the higher the ask, while informed buyers (who trade only when the sum exceeds the ask) take more from us. The net is roughly flat between 14.8 and 15, so I'm quoting 14.8, with the mirror bid at 7.2. The bid side has the same profile because the sum distribution is symmetric about 11.

{"bid": 7.2, "ask": 14.8}

Dice 1 and 2 are known (4 and 6), so the settlement is 10 + die 3, uniform on 11 to 16. The public fair value is 13.5. This is the last quote, so inventory doesn't matter beyond this trade.

Expected profit from the ask is 0.65·P(V>a)·(a−13.5) from noise traders, plus 0.35·E[(a−S)·1{S>a}] from the informed trader. The noise term peaks near a ≈ 16.8, where the informed trader can't profitably buy because S ≤ 16. The distribution is symmetric about 13.5, so the best bid is 13.5 − 3.3 = 10.2, where the informed trader can't profitably sell because S ≥ 11. The trade history reveals nothing new, since no trades is consistent with S anywhere in 11 to 16.

{"bid": 10.2, "ask": 16.8}

**Estimate:** Die 1 is 5, so the sum is 5 + d2 + d3. Before any dice were revealed, the fair value was 10.5. Trade 2 (a buy at 12.4) is evidence for a higher sum, since an informed buyer only buys when S ≥ 13. Updating on that gives a fair value of about 12.8. The sale at 6.6 in quote 1 was only possible from a noise trader, so it carries little information.

**Spread:** Informed traders are more likely to hit a quote when the sum is far from 12.8, so I widen the spread to cover adverse selection. Break-even is roughly 11.95 on the bid and 13.5 on the ask. I quote slightly outside those points on each side.

{"bid": 11.9, "ask": 13.6}

Public fair value: die 1 = 5 is known, and the other two dice average 3.5 each, so E[sum] = 5 + 3.5 + 3.5 = 12.0. Recent trades are roughly balanced, so I'm keeping 12.0.

I quote a wide spread because informed traders are a minority (35%) and noise traders' valuations spread around 12 with sd 4.4, so trades far from fair value still earn an edge. With a +1 long position, I shade both sides down to encourage sales to the market and discourage further buying from us. Bid 7.5 and ask 15.0 are roughly symmetric around 12 with that downward shift.

{"bid": 7.5, "ask": 15.0}

Fair value is 1 + 3.5 + 3.5 = 8.0, since die 1 is revealed and dice 2 and 3 are unknown. A quote centred on 8 is the right starting point.

I checked half-spreads h around 8 by comparing expected profit from noise traders with expected adverse-selection loss from the informed trader. The net edge peaks near h = 3, which gives a bid of 5 and an ask of 11. That matches the previous quote, so I'm keeping it.

{"bid": 5, "ask": 11}

Die 1 = 1 and die 2 = 6 are known, so the public fair value is 1 + 6 + 3.5 = 10.5. The informed counterparty knows the sum S, which ranges over 8 to 13.

Optimizing the ask against the mix of informed and noise buyers, the expected profit per quote is about 0.33 at an ask of 13.0. Asks at 11.0 or below lose money on average, because informed buyers take the sells that are most costly to us. The bid is symmetric around 10.5, so the best bid is 8.0, with the same expected profit of about 0.33. Position is 0, so no inventory adjustment is needed.

{"bid": 8.0, "ask": 13.0}

Fair value is 4 + 4 + 3.5 = 11.5, since die 3 is uniform on 1 to 6. An informed counterparty knows the sum S = 8 + die 3, which ranges over 9 to 14.

A quote at 14 ask and 9 bid gets no informed trades, because S can never exceed 14 or fall below 9. Noise traders still trade at a 2.5 edge on each side, so this is the best spread I checked. Tighter quotes such as 13/10 or 12/11 lose money to informed flow. Position -2 doesn't change the expected-value calculation for a risk-neutral objective.

{"bid": 9, "ask": 14}

The fair value before any reveals is the expected sum of three dice, 10.5. The informed trader only trades against me when the true value is on the wrong side of my quote, so the optimal spread trades off adverse selection against the noise traders' flow.

For an ask at `a`, my expected profit per quote is roughly 0.8·(a − 10.5)·P(noise valuation > a) − 0.2·E[(V − a)⁺], where V is the dice sum. Evaluating this over the discrete sum distribution gives a maximum near a ≈ 14.1 to 14.2, with a value of about 0.56. By symmetry of the dice sum around 10.5, the matching bid is about 6.9.

{"bid": 6.9, "ask": 14.1}

Die 1 is 5, so the public fair value is 5 + 3.5 + 3.5 = 12.0. The settlement is symmetric around 12, so I'm quoting symmetrically with no inventory skew (position is 0).

Adverse selection sets the spread. An informed buyer arrives when the settlement is above my ask, and it carries information. Solving for the ask where my expected value given a buy equals the ask gives about 12.4 to 12.5. By symmetry the bid is about 11.5 to 11.6. Bids round down and asks round up, so I quote 11.5 and 12.5.

{"bid": 11.5, "ask": 12.5}

Die 1 is revealed as 5, and die 2 won't be revealed until before stage 3, so the public fair value is 5 + 3.5 + 3.5 = 12. My position is flat, so there's no inventory skew to apply.

Informed traders know the sum S = 5 + d2 + d3, which ranges from 7 to 17 with standard deviation about 2.4. Noise traders' valuations have standard deviation 4.4, so they are much more dispersed. Against that mix, a tight spread loses money to informed flow. Working through the expected profit per side, with the ask at 12 + h and the bid at 12 − h, the profit peaks around h ≈ 3 to 3.5, at roughly 0.58 per quote. Rounding to the 0.1 grid gives a bid of 8.7 (rounded down from 8.7) and an ask of 15.3 (rounded up from 15.25).

{"bid": 8.7, "ask": 15.3}

Before any reveal, the fair value is the expected sum of three fair dice, 3 × 3.5 = 10.5. Noise traders value the contract at 10.5 plus N(0, 4.4). Their trades are profitable for me in expectation, while informed trades are not.

I estimated the expected profit from the ask as a function of its distance d from 10.5, using the exact sum distribution. Noise-trader gains rise with d, while informed-trader losses stay small, so the net peaks near d ≈ 3.5–4 (about 0.55 per quote). The prior quotes of 7 and 14 were close to this but slightly too tight, and they produced no trades. Quoting symmetrically at d = 4 gives bid 6.5 and ask 14.5.

{"bid": 6.5, "ask": 14.5}

Die 1 is 3, so the fair value is 3 + 3.5 + 3.5 = 10, and the settlement distribution is symmetric around 10. Position is 0, so there is no inventory skew.

The breakeven ask, where the noise-trader gain balances the informed-trader loss, is about 10.45. Rounding up to the 0.1 grid gives 10.5, which is marginally profitable. By symmetry, the breakeven bid is about 9.55, and rounding down gives 9.5.

{"bid": 9.5, "ask": 10.5}

Die 1 is 3, so the public fair value is 3 + 3.5 + 3.5 = 10. The unknown part of the sum (dice 2 and 3) has mean 7 and standard deviation about 2.4.

I set the quotes by comparing expected profit per quote across half-spreads. Each side earns roughly 0.8 × (distance from 10) × P(noise trade) and loses 0.2 × E[adverse move] to informed traders. Half-spreads near 3.5 come out best, and the exact discrete sum distribution confirms this. Bid 6.5 and ask 13.5 are roughly symmetric around 10 and each earns about 0.58 per side in expectation. The trade history is roughly balanced, so I'm not shifting the midpoint.

{"bid": 6.5, "ask": 13.5}

Die 1 = 3 and die 2 = 4, so the settlement is 7 + D3 with D3 uniform on 1 to 6. The public fair value is 10.5. The informed counterparty (probability 0.2) buys only if the settlement is above my ask and sells only if it is below my bid. Noise traders (probability 0.8) trade on valuations centred on 10.5 with standard deviation 4.4.

For an ask A, the expected profit is 0.8 · P(V > A) · (A − 10.5) minus the informed adverse-selection loss. The informed loss is zero for A ≥ 13, since the settlement cannot exceed 13. The noise term is maximised near A ≈ 13.8, giving about 0.60. The bid side is symmetric around 10.5, so the best bid is about 7.2, also worth about 0.60. Position is 0 and I am risk-neutral, so the sides add independently and I can quote the one-shot optimum.

{"bid": 7.2, "ask": 13.8}

Die 1 = 3 and die 2 = 4 are revealed, so the settlement is 7 + die 3, uniform on {8,...,13}. The public fair value is 10.5.

The informed trader only trades when its valuation is beyond my quote. Its valuation is at most 13, so a bid of 7.2 or an ask of 13.8 cannot be hit by an informed trader. Against noise traders (sd 4.4), the expected profit per side is (quote distance from 10.5) × P(noise trader trades). Maximising that gives a distance of about 3.3, so the best quotes are about 7.2 and 13.8. This matches quote 7.

{"bid": 7.2, "ask": 13.8}

Die 3 is still hidden, so the public fair value is 3 + 4 + 3.5 = 10.5. The sum can only be 8 to 13, and this is the last quote, so there is no future inventory to manage.

Against noise traders (std 4.4 around 10.5), the ask that maximises expected profit is about 14: higher asks earn more per trade, and the informed trader never buys above 13. The bid is symmetric about 10.5, so the best bid is about 7.

{"bid": 7.0, "ask": 14.0}

Die 1 is 6 and die 2 is still hidden, so the public fair value is 6 + 3.5 + 3.5 = 13. My position is flat, so I'll quote symmetrically around 13.

Against noise traders (valuation ~ N(13, 3)), a wider spread earns more per trade but trades less often. Against the informed trader (who trades whenever the true total 8–18 is outside my quotes), a wider spread reduces adverse selection. Balancing these, the expected profit on the ask side peaks around 15.5 to 16 (about 0.37 at 15.5 versus 0.36 at 16). The distribution is symmetric around 13, so the bid goes at 26 minus the ask, which is 10.5.

{"bid": 10.5, "ask": 15.5}

Public fair value is 6 + 3.5 + 3.5 = 13. Die 3 and the second die are still hidden, so the sum is uncertain. Noise traders on either side have zero expected edge at 13, but informed traders (20%) would trade against any quote too close to 13, so the quote needs a spread.

For a symmetric spread of ±d around 13, the expected edge per quote on each side is roughly 0.8·P(noise trades)·d minus the expected loss to informed traders. Evaluating d from 2 to 4 with the exact sum distribution (8 to 18), the best edge is at about d = 2.5, where both sides give roughly 0.37 per quote. Bid 10.5 and ask 15.5 are symmetric about 13, both valid tick prices, and the bid is below the ask.

{"bid": 10.5, "ask": 15.5}

The public fair value is 6 + 2 + 3.5 = 11.5, since die 3 is still unrevealed. Noise traders centre on 11.5 with standard deviation 3, and the informed trader's value ranges over 9 to 14 (uniform).

For a bid B, my expected edge is roughly 0.8·Φ((B−11.5)/3)·(11.5−B) from noise traders, plus 0.2·(1/6)·Σ(v−B) over values v<B from informed traders. For an ask A, the calculation mirrors this. The edge peaks near B≈9.0 and A≈14.0, with both at about 0.40 per side. Since settlement profit is marked at the fair value, holding a short of 2 doesn't cost anything in expectation, so I'm not skewing for inventory.

{"bid": 9.0, "ask": 14.0}

Die 1 is 5, and die 2 and die 3 are unrevealed, so the public fair value is 5 + 3.5 + 3.5 = 12. My position is flat, and the distribution of the sum is symmetric around 12, so I quote symmetrically.

Working through the expected profit from each side, using noise traders at N(12, 3) and informed traders at 20%, the profit is roughly flat between half-spreads of 2.5 and 3. I'll take a half-spread of 2.5, which gives a bid of 9.5 and an ask of 14.5.

{"bid": 9.5, "ask": 14.5}

Die 1 is 5, so the public fair value is 5 + 3.5 + 3.5 = 12. The sum ranges from 7 to 17 and is symmetric around 12.

Noise traders have valuations centred on 12 with standard deviation 3, so their trades carry no adverse selection. Informed traders trade only when the true sum is past my price, so they hurt me. Choosing the ask to maximise expected profit per quote, with the noise-trader and informed-trader terms weighted by their probabilities, gives an optimum near 14.5. The bid is symmetric about 12, so the optimum is near 9.5. This matches quote 4, and the market has not changed.

{"bid": 9.5, "ask": 14.5}

Die 1 is revealed as 5. The two unrevealed dice have expected sum 7, so the public fair value is 5 + 7 = 12. Noise valuations are centred there, with sd 3.

The informed traders know the exact total V, which ranges from 7 to 17 and is symmetric about 12. Working through the expected profit per side, the ask near 14.5 and the bid near 9.5 maximise the market maker's expected profit, since the noise-trader spread gain outweighs the adverse-selection loss at those levels. I'm keeping the same quotes as quotes 4 and 5.

{"bid": 9.5, "ask": 14.5}

Die 1 = 5 and die 2 = 6 are known, so the public fair value is 11 + 3.5 = 14.5. The settlement value is 11 + die 3, which is uniform on {12,...,17}. Informed traders (20%) buy only when that value exceeds my ask and sell only when it is below my bid. Noise traders (80%) trade on their own noisy valuation, so their trades carry no information about die 3.

I searched for the ask and bid that maximise expected profit from one quote under this model. Asks near 16.9 and bids near 12.0 come out best, since the informed-trader adverse selection is small at those levels (only the top outcome, 17, can trigger an informed buy). Holding a neutral position is reasonable with three quotes left.

{"bid": 12.0, "ask": 16.9}

Public fair value: die 1 = 5 and die 2 = 6 are known, and die 3 is uniform on 1 to 6 (mean 3.5), so the fair value is 14.5. The informed trader knows the exact sum (12 to 17), and noise traders value it at 14.5 ± 3.

Expected profit per side, for a given quote, is 0.8 × P(noise trader trades) × (edge over 14.5) plus the informed-trader term. Among tick-sized quotes, the ask at 17.0 and the bid at 12.0 come out near the maximum, about 0.40 each. Both are symmetric around 14.5, and my position is flat, so there's no inventory skew to add.

{"bid": 12.0, "ask": 17.0}

Expected settlement value is 18.425 (die 1 mean 6.75, die 2 mean 6.8, die 3 mean 4.875). The total standard deviation is about 6.5, similar to the noise-trader spread.

With a spread of d on each side, the expected profit per side is roughly d·P(noise trader crosses) minus the expected loss to informed traders, E[(V−A)⁺]. Evaluating that numerically, the expected profit is near its maximum at d ≈ 7, and it is flat between about 7 and 8. Rounding gives a bid of 11.4 and an ask of 25.5.

{"bid": 11.4, "ask": 25.5}

Settlement = die 1 + die 2 + 3. Die 1 is uniform with mean 6.75, and die 2 is loaded with mean 6.8, so the public fair value is 16.55. I'm long 3, so the bid is pulled and only the ask matters.

Selling at ask A earns A − V against an informed buyer (who buys only when V > A) and A − 16.55 against a noise buyer (who buys when their valuation, ~N(16.55, 6.5), exceeds A). Choosing A to maximise the expected profit from these trades gives an optimum at A = 24.0. Its derivative is positive just below 24 and negative just above.

{"bid": null, "ask": 24}

Die 1 has mean 54/8 = 6.75, so the public fair value is 6.75 + 1 + 3 = 10.75. The settlement value V is die 1 + 4, which is one of {4, 5, 7, 12, 13, 14, 15, 16} with equal probability.

I'm long 3 and can only quote an ask. The main cost is adverse selection: informed buyers only take the ask when V exceeds it. Weighing noise-trader gains against informed-trader losses across asks, the expected gain peaks near 16. At an ask of 16, the informed buyer never trades (V = 16 is not above 16), so the expected gain is about +0.55 per unit. At 15 or 17 it is lower, and at 11 or 12 it is negative. The bid is pulled at +3, so I quote null there.

{"bid": null, "ask": 16.0}

Fair value before any reveal is the expected sum: 6.75 (die 1) + 6.8 (die 2) + 4.875 (die 3) = 18.425. The settlement SD is about 6.5, the same as the noise-trader SD.

Setting the ask at μ + s, the first-order condition balances the informed buyers' adverse-selection cost against the noise-trader spread income. Using the exact tail probabilities of the sum, the best ask is 26.0. For the bid, the best level is about 10.92, which rounds down to 10.9.

{"bid": 10.9, "ask": 26.0}

With no information revealed, the public fair value is the expected sum: E[die 1] + E[die 2] + E[die 3] = 3.75 + 2.5 + 6.25 = 12.5. The total standard deviation is about 5.8, matching the noise traders' spread.

Quoting symmetrically around 12.5 with a half-spread d, the expected profit per quote is roughly 2d·Φ(-d/5.8) − 5.8·φ(d/5.8). This is maximised near d ≈ 7, which balances the spread earned from noise traders against losses to informed traders. I'm using the normal approximation here, since the exact discrete distribution would take much longer to work through.

{"bid": 5.5, "ask": 19.5}

Fair value before any reveal is the mean of the sum: 3.75 + 2.5 + 6.25 = 12.5. The noise-trader spread is about 5.8, and the total variance is about 33.6, so the sd is roughly 5.8 as well.

Against informed flow, the spread that maximises expected profit on each side is about 6.8 from fair value, which gives a bid near 5.7 and an ask near 19.3. This matches quote 1, which was posted at 5.5 and 19.5. Since settlement profit is risk-neutral, my +1 position doesn't change the expected value of these prices, so I'm not skewing for inventory.

{"bid": 5.7, "ask": 19.3}

The sum of three unrevealed dice has mean 3.75 + 2.5 + 6.25 = 12.5. The first quote's trade is informative, though. Someone sold to us at 5.5, which is much more likely if the sum is low, so the posterior fair value drops to roughly 9.3. Quote 2's no-trade result pushes the estimate slightly further.

With that posterior, an informed counterparty (who knows the sum) trades against any quote whose price is wrong, which pushes the optimal spread wide. Balancing noise-trader profit against informed-trader losses gives a bid near 2.0 and an ask near 17. Since I'm long +1, I'm rounding the ask down slightly to 16.9 to favour selling.

{"bid": 2.0, "ask": 16.9}

Fair value from public information is about 17.9. Without any reveal, the expected sum is 3.75 + 2.5 + 6.25 = 12.5. The history shifts that: quote 1 was bought at 19.5 (a buyer valued it above 19.5), and quote 2 saw no trade (valuations fell between 11.6 and 21). Updating on both gives a posterior mean of about 17.9, which is also the noise traders' centre.

Quoting against that belief, the ask maximises expected profit where the marginal gain from noise buyers equals the marginal informed-buyer loss. That gives about 23.2. The bid, by the same logic, sits near 11.0. Position -1 is well inside the ±4 limits, so it doesn't change the quote.

{"bid": 11.0, "ask": 23.2}

Fair value is the expected sum given public information. Die 1 = 8 is revealed. Die 2 is unrevealed, with expected value 0.8 + 0.2 + 1.2 + 2.4 = 4.6. Die 3 has expected value (7+7+8+10)/4 = 8. So the public fair value is 8 + 4.6 + 8 = 20.6.

Die 2 and die 3 together give a standard deviation of about 3, and noise traders add 3.6 more, so informed flow is a real risk. Position is flat, so I'll quote symmetrically around 20.6 with a half-spread of about 2.8, slightly tighter than the 4.8 spread on the last quote.

{"bid": 17.8, "ask": 23.4}

Before any reveal, the expected settlement is 7 (die 1) + 4.6 (die 2) + 8 (die 3) = 19.6, with standard deviation about 3.6. That matches the noise-trader spread, so the quotes from quote 1 (16 / 23.2) were roughly balanced around fair value.

Since I'm short one unit, I'll keep the ask at 23.2 so I don't add to the short, and nudge the bid up slightly to 16.4 to make it easier to buy back.

{"bid": 16.4, "ask": 23.2}

Public fair value: die 1 is revealed as 11. Die 2 has expected value 4.6 and die 3 has expected value 8, so the fair value is 11 + 4.6 + 8 = 23.6.

Against noise traders (valuation about N(23.6, 3.6)), each side earns its distance from fair value times the chance of a trade. Against informed traders, each side loses the expected amount by which the settlement value sits beyond the quote. Balancing these gives a fairly wide, roughly symmetric quote, with the bid near 20 and the ask near 27. Since I'm short 1, I nudge the bid up slightly to make buys from me more likely and keep the ask near its optimum.

{"bid": 20.3, "ask": 27.0}

Fair value is 16: the dice means are 5.75, 5, and 5.25, which sum to 16. Die 2 stays hidden until settlement, so 16 is the fair value for now.

Noise traders are symmetric around 16 and add no information, but informed traders (20%) trade whenever the true value is outside my quotes. Balancing the spread against that adverse selection puts the ask about 5.8 above fair value and the bid about 5.8 below, so I'll tighten slightly from 10.5/21.5 to 10.2/21.8.

{"bid": 10.2, "ask": 21.8}

Die 3 is revealed as 7. The public fair value is 5.75 + 5 + 7 = 17.75, so the noise traders' valuations are centred there with SD 6.6.

Working through the expected profit per quote, with noise traders trading at my prices and informed traders trading whenever the true value is past my quote, the best ask is about 23 and the best bid is about 12.2. Position +1 is well inside the ±4 limit, and I'm treating inventory as neutral since I'm risk-neutral.

{"bid": 12.2, "ask": 23.0}

Die 2 is the only unknown. Its six faces are equally likely with mean 5, so the public fair value is 5 + 7 + 5 = 17. The total can only be 13, 14, 15, 16, 20 or 24, and the informed trader knows which.

Against noise traders (valuation about N(17, 6.6)), the expected edge from each side is positive at these prices. Against the informed trader, the edge is reduced by how far the realised total sits beyond my quote. Working through the grid, the best bid is about 12.0 and the best ask is about 22.5. Position +2 is within limits and the objective is expected profit, so I'm not skewing the quote for inventory.

{"bid": 12.0, "ask": 22.5}

The public fair value is the sum of the die means: 5.75 + 5 + 5.25 = 16. With no information revealed, both the noise traders and the informed traders are centred on 16, so I quote symmetrically.

For a half-spread d around 16, the expected profit per side is about 0.8·d·P(V > ask) minus 0.2 times the expected amount by which the informed trader's value exceeds the ask. Setting the derivative to zero gives 1 − Φ(z) = 0.8·z·φ(z) with z = d/6.6, which solves to z ≈ 0.88, so d ≈ 5.8. That puts the ask at 21.8 and the bid at 10.2.

{"bid": 10.2, "ask": 21.8}

Die 3 is 6, so the public fair value is E[die 1] + E[die 2] + 6 = 5.75 + 5 + 6 = 16.75. Die 1 and die 2 are both still hidden, so the settlement value V has a spread of roughly 5 around that mean. Noise traders are centred on 16.75 with sd 6.6, which is wider than V.

I chose each side to maximise expected profit per quote, treating each side separately and ignoring the small inventory effect (expected profit is unaffected by the -1 position). For the ask, the expected gain from noise buyers, 0.8 × P(X > A) × (A − 16.75), minus the informed-buyer loss, 0.2 × E[(V − A)⁺], peaks around 22.4 to 22.5. For the bid, the mirror calculation peaks around 11.0 to 11.2.

{"bid": 11.1, "ask": 22.4}

The public fair value is the expected sum: die 1 averages 8, die 2 averages 5.33, and die 3 averages 6.5, so E[V] ≈ 19.83. No trade has happened and nothing is revealed, so the public estimate is unchanged.

Quoting against this mean costs money when informed traders (35%) pick off the side that is wrong. For a sell at ask A, the expected profit is about 0.65·P(noise buys)·(A − 19.83) − 0.35·E[(V − A)⁺ | informed]. Using a normal approximation with sd ≈ 5.74, this breaks even near A ≈ 22. The bid side is symmetric, so breakeven is near 19.83 − 2.17 ≈ 17.7. Rounding bids down and asks up gives bid 17.6 and ask 22.0.

{"bid": 17.6, "ask": 22.0}

**Fair value:** Die 1 has mean 8 and die 2 has mean 16/3 ≈ 5.33, which gives an expected sum of 8 + 5.33 + 9 (die 3 revealed) ≈ 22.3. The two earlier no-trade quotes carry little information, so I'm centering on 22.3.

**Spread:** Uncertainty about the settlement value has standard deviation about 4.4. Informed traders (35%) trade against any quote that is wrong, and noise traders (65%, sd 5.7) trade whenever their valuation clears a quote. A rough one-shot model of expected profit as a function of half-spread peaks around a half-spread of 5. That gives a bid of 17.3 and an ask of 27.3. Position is flat, so there is no inventory skew.

