The sum has expected value 14 (die 1 averages 6, die 2 averages 8), and die 1 stays hidden until settlement, so the public fair value is 14. Noise traders value the contract at N(14, 4.7²). Informed traders (20%) know the exact sum, so I need a spread wide enough to cover their adverse selection.

I checked symmetric half-spreads d around 14. For each, I compared the expected profit from noise-trader flow with the expected loss to informed flow. The net edge peaks around d ≈ 4 to 4.5 (about 0.42 per side), and the sum's distribution makes the bid and ask sides roughly equal at d = 4. I'll quote bid 10 and ask 18.

{"bid": 10, "ask": 18}

Fair value before any cards are revealed is 21: three cards with mean 7 each, sd about 6.35 for the sum.

With no inventory yet, I'll quote symmetrically around 21. A tight spread loses money here. Informed traders pick off stale quotes more often than the noise flow pays me, so the spread needs to be wide. My rough model puts the break-even near a half-spread of 4 and the expected edge peaking around a half-spread of 7 to 8, where it is about +0.5 per quote. I'll quote a half-spread of 7.5.

{"bid": 13.5, "ask": 28.5}

The public fair value is the expected sum: die 1 averages 6.33, die 2 averages 6.25, and die 3 averages 6.33, so the fair value is about 18.92. Nothing is revealed yet, so this is the best public estimate.

I set the quotes by balancing noise-trader flow, which is uninformative and earns the spread, against informed flow, which trades only when the true sum is far from my quotes. The noise standard deviation of 9.8 is much larger than the sum's standard deviation of about 6.5, so informed trades are rare at moderate distances. Working through the first-order condition with normal approximations to the sum's distribution puts the optimal half-spread at roughly 9.5 on each side. That gives a bid near 9.4 and an ask near 28.4, close to the previous quotes.

{"bid": 9.4, "ask": 28.4}

Die 2 is still hidden, so the public fair value is 7 + E[die 2] = 7 + (1 + 2 + 11 + 11)/4 = 13.25. Die 2 sums to 8, 9, 18 or 18, so the informed buyer only buys if my ask is below 18.

